#
#       Composed onto arch/riscv, for machines that do unaligned access in
#       hardware.
#
#               sh build.sh arch/riscv debug_none riscv_fast
#
#       Not in arch/riscv itself, and for the reason that profile already
#       gives about Zbb: it names a VisionFive and an Unmatched as targets, and
#       the U74 in both of those traps an unaligned access to firmware, which
#       emulates it an instruction at a time. Turning this on there would not
#       be slower at the margin, it would be catastrophic -- Kconfig's own
#       words for that case are "much more slowly, or will not be able to run
#       at all". A floor nothing here can boot is not a floor.
#
#       What it is worth where it does apply, measured on the built image:
#
#       riscv64 has no CONFIG_HAVE_EFFICIENT_UNALIGNED_ACCESS, so every
#       get_unaligned and put_unaligned in the kernel expands to a byte-wise
#       packed-struct access. That is not a nuance at one call site, it is the
#       shape of every one of them. memcpy_toio spends eighteen memory
#       operations moving eight bytes, routed through a stack frame it
#       allocates for the purpose, where memset_io beside it -- which needs no
#       get_unaligned -- emits one store per eight bytes and is already right.
#
#       It also gates DCACHE_WORD_ACCESS, which x86_64 and arm64 both have and
#       riscv64 does not. Without it full_name_hash reads one byte at a time,
#       so every path lookup on the system hashes bytewise.
#
#       QEMU's virt machine does unaligned access in hardware, as do the newer
#       cores, so this is the profile to build with when that is what is being
#       run on.
#
CONFIG_NONPORTABLE=y
CONFIG_RISCV_EFFICIENT_UNALIGNED_ACCESS=y
